WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:04.670 --> 00:15.480
CHAIR YELLEN.

00:15.480 --> 00:22.960
Good afternoon.

00:22.960 --> 00:26.940
Today the Federal Open Market
Committee reaffirmed the current

00:26.940 --> 00:29.770
0 to one quarter
percent target range

00:29.770 --> 00:32.210
for the federal funds rate.

00:32.210 --> 00:35.670
Since the Committee
last met in April,

00:35.670 --> 00:38.520
the pace of job gains
has picked up

00:38.520 --> 00:43.370
and labor market conditions
have improved somewhat further.

00:43.370 --> 00:48.170
Inflation has continued to run
below our longer-run objective,

00:48.170 --> 00:51.220
but some of the downward
pressure on inflation resulting

00:51.220 --> 00:56.960
from earlier sharp declines
in energy prices is abating.

00:56.960 --> 01:00.260
The Committee continues to
judge that the first increase

01:00.260 --> 01:03.300
in the federal funds
rate will be appropriate

01:03.300 --> 01:06.870
when it has seen further
improvement in the labor market

01:06.870 --> 01:11.440
and is reasonably confident
that inflation will move back

01:11.440 --> 01:15.460
to its 2 percent objective
over the medium term.

01:15.460 --> 01:19.230
At our meeting that ended
today, the Committee concluded

01:19.230 --> 01:23.600
that these conditions have
not yet been achieved.

01:23.600 --> 01:26.600
It remains the case that the
Committee will determine the

01:26.600 --> 01:30.350
timing of the initial increase
in the federal funds rate

01:30.350 --> 01:34.500
on a meeting-by-meeting basis,
depending on its assessment

01:34.500 --> 01:38.830
of incoming economic
information and its implications

01:38.830 --> 01:41.210
for the economic outlook.

01:41.210 --> 01:43.430
Let me emphasize
that the importance

01:43.430 --> 01:47.830
of the initial increase
should not be overstated:

01:47.830 --> 01:52.220
The stance of monetary policy
will likely remain highly

01:52.220 --> 01:56.890
accommodative for quite some
time after the initial increase

01:56.890 --> 01:59.480
in the federal funds
rate in order

01:59.480 --> 02:03.090
to support continued
progress toward our objectives

02:03.090 --> 02:07.080
of maximum employment
and 2 percent inflation.

02:07.080 --> 02:09.860
I will come back to
today's policy decision

02:09.860 --> 02:12.050
in a few moments,
but first I would

02:12.050 --> 02:16.630
like to review recent economic
developments and the outlook.

02:16.630 --> 02:21.450
The U.S. economy hit a soft
patch earlier this year;

02:21.450 --> 02:23.630
real gross domestic
product looks

02:23.630 --> 02:27.830
to have changed little
in the first quarter.

02:27.830 --> 02:30.880
Growth in household
spending slowed,

02:30.880 --> 02:33.950
business fixed investment
edged down,

02:33.950 --> 02:38.530
and net exports were a
substantial drag on growth.

02:38.530 --> 02:41.000
Part of this weakness
was likely the result

02:41.000 --> 02:43.600
of transitory factors.

02:43.600 --> 02:45.820
Despite the soft first quarter,

02:45.820 --> 02:49.350
the fundamentals underlying
household spending appear

02:49.350 --> 02:54.340
favorable, and consumer
sentiment remains solid.

02:54.340 --> 02:58.010
Looking ahead, the Committee
still expects a moderate pace

02:58.010 --> 03:02.700
of GDP growth, with
continuing job gains

03:02.700 --> 03:07.480
and lower energy prices
supporting household spending.

03:07.480 --> 03:08.730
The labor market data

03:08.730 --> 03:11.980
so far this year have shown
further progress toward our

03:11.980 --> 03:15.190
objective of maximum employment,

03:15.190 --> 03:18.910
although at a slower
pace than late last year.

03:18.910 --> 03:22.390
Over the past three months,
job gains have averaged

03:22.390 --> 03:27.590
about 210,000 per month,
down from an average pace

03:27.590 --> 03:33.370
of 280,000 per month over
the second half of last year,

03:33.370 --> 03:35.970
but still well above
the pace consistent

03:35.970 --> 03:39.180
with trend labor force growth.

03:39.180 --> 03:44.020
Although the unemployment
rate, at 5.5 percent in May,

03:44.020 --> 03:47.570
was unchanged from the latest
reading available at the time

03:47.570 --> 03:52.130
of our April meeting, the
labor force participation rate

03:52.130 --> 03:53.540
edged up.

03:53.540 --> 03:55.400
A broader measure
of unemployment

03:55.400 --> 04:00.710
that includes individuals who
want and are available to work

04:00.710 --> 04:04.630
but have not actively
searched recently and people

04:04.630 --> 04:06.020
who are working part time

04:06.020 --> 04:11.530
but would rather work full
time has continued to improve.

04:11.530 --> 04:15.080
But it seems likely that
some cyclical weakness

04:15.080 --> 04:17.610
in the labor market remains:

04:17.610 --> 04:22.090
The participation rate
remains below most estimates

04:22.090 --> 04:24.580
of its underlying trend,

04:24.580 --> 04:28.440
involuntary part-time
employment remains elevated,

04:28.440 --> 04:32.760
and wage growth remains
relatively subdued.

04:32.760 --> 04:36.820
So, although progress
clearly has been achieved,

04:36.820 --> 04:40.700
room for further
improvement remains.

04:40.700 --> 04:44.930
Inflation has continued to run
below our longer-run objective,

04:44.930 --> 04:48.620
in part reflecting
lower energy prices.

04:48.620 --> 04:53.250
Declines in import prices have
also restrained inflation.

04:53.250 --> 04:58.140
However, energy prices appear
to have stabilized recently.

04:58.140 --> 05:02.250
My colleagues and I continue
to expect that as the effects

05:02.250 --> 05:05.470
of these transitory
factors dissipate

05:05.470 --> 05:08.500
and as the labor
market improves further,

05:08.500 --> 05:11.840
inflation will gradually move
back toward our 2 percent

05:11.840 --> 05:15.060
objective over the medium term.

05:15.060 --> 05:16.270
Market-based measures

05:16.270 --> 05:20.680
of inflation compensation remain
low, though they have risen some

05:20.680 --> 05:25.160
from their levels earlier this
year, and survey-based measures

05:25.160 --> 05:27.530
of longer-term inflation
expectations have

05:27.530 --> 05:29.560
remained stable.

05:29.560 --> 05:30.900
The Committee will continue

05:30.900 --> 05:34.930
to monitor inflation
developments carefully.

05:34.930 --> 05:37.410
This assessment of the
outlook is reflected

05:37.410 --> 05:40.740
in the individual economic
projections submitted

05:40.740 --> 05:45.150
for this meeting by
FOMC participants.

05:45.150 --> 05:49.360
As always, each participant's
projections are conditioned

05:49.360 --> 05:53.960
on his or her own view of
appropriate monetary policy.

05:53.960 --> 05:55.840
For economic growth,

05:55.840 --> 05:59.520
participants reduced their
projections for this year,

05:59.520 --> 06:03.650
in line with the disappointing
data for the first quarter.

06:03.650 --> 06:06.340
The central tendency of
the growth projections

06:06.340 --> 06:13.540
for 2015 is now 1.8 to 2.0
percent, down a little more

06:13.540 --> 06:17.760
than half a percentage point
from the March projections.

06:17.760 --> 06:24.860
The central tendency rises to
2.4 to 2.7 percent next year,

06:24.860 --> 06:29.130
somewhat above estimates of
the longer-run growth rate.

06:29.130 --> 06:30.960
The unemployment
rate projections

06:30.960 --> 06:34.200
for this year are a little
higher than in March.

06:34.200 --> 06:36.830
At the end of this year,
the central tendency

06:36.830 --> 06:43.170
for the unemployment rate
stands at 5.2 to 5.3 percent,

06:43.170 --> 06:45.720
a bit above participants'
estimates

06:45.720 --> 06:49.710
of the longer-run
normal unemployment rate.

06:49.710 --> 06:53.160
Committee participants generally
see the unemployment rate

06:53.160 --> 06:59.280
declining a little further over
the course of 2016 and 2017.

06:59.280 --> 07:03.770
Finally, FOMC participants
project inflation

07:03.770 --> 07:08.540
to be quite low this year,
largely reflecting lower energy

07:08.540 --> 07:11.630
and non-energy import prices.

07:11.630 --> 07:15.040
The central tendency of
the inflation projections

07:15.040 --> 07:20.300
for this year is below 1
percent, unchanged since March.

07:20.300 --> 07:25.190
As the transitory factors
holding down inflation abate,

07:25.190 --> 07:31.680
the central tendency rises to
1.6 to 1.9 percent next year

07:31.680 --> 07:36.140
and to 1.9 to 2.0
percent in 2017.

07:36.140 --> 07:40.370
Returning to monetary
policy, as I noted,

07:40.370 --> 07:43.600
the Committee reaffirmed
its view that the current 0

07:43.600 --> 07:45.880
to one quarter percent
target range

07:45.880 --> 07:49.870
for the federal funds
rate remains appropriate.

07:49.870 --> 07:52.670
As we said in our
statement, the decision

07:52.670 --> 07:56.550
to raise the target range
will depend on our assessment

07:56.550 --> 08:00.730
of realized and expected
progress toward our objectives

08:00.730 --> 08:04.830
of maximum employment
and 2 percent inflation.

08:04.830 --> 08:07.720
We continue to base that
assessment on a wide range

08:07.720 --> 08:10.190
of information, including
measures

08:10.190 --> 08:12.540
of labor market conditions,

08:12.540 --> 08:15.020
indicators of inflation
pressures

08:15.020 --> 08:18.910
and inflation expectations,
and readings on financial

08:18.910 --> 08:21.300
and international developments.

08:21.300 --> 08:25.000
And we continue to anticipate
that it will be appropriate

08:25.000 --> 08:28.470
to raise the target range
for the federal funds rate

08:28.470 --> 08:30.650
when the Committee has
seen further improvement

08:30.650 --> 08:33.820
in the labor market and
is reasonably confident

08:33.820 --> 08:35.910
that inflation will move back

08:35.910 --> 08:40.220
to its 2 percent objective
over the medium term.

08:40.220 --> 08:43.370
On both of these
fronts, as I noted,

08:43.370 --> 08:46.470
we have seen some progress.

08:46.470 --> 08:50.340
Even so, the Committee judged
that economic conditions do not

08:50.340 --> 08:54.490
yet warrant an increase
in the federal funds rate.

08:54.490 --> 08:57.180
While the Committee views
the disappointing economic

08:57.180 --> 09:02.360
performance in the first
quarter as largely transitory,

09:02.360 --> 09:06.510
my colleagues and I would like
to see more decisive evidence

09:06.510 --> 09:10.280
that a moderate pace of economic
growth will be sustained,

09:10.280 --> 09:14.900
so that conditions in the labor
market will continue to improve

09:14.900 --> 09:18.720
and inflation will
move back to 2 percent.

09:18.720 --> 09:22.350
Once we begin to remove
policy accommodation,

09:22.350 --> 09:27.700
we continue to expect that-as
we say in our statement-"even

09:27.700 --> 09:31.260
after employment and inflation
are near mandate-consistent

09:31.260 --> 09:35.780
levels, economic conditions
may, for some time,

09:35.780 --> 09:39.690
warrant keeping the target
federal funds rate below levels

09:39.690 --> 09:43.350
the Committee views as
normal in the longer run."

09:43.350 --> 09:47.470
In other words, although
policy will be data dependent,

09:47.470 --> 09:52.340
economic conditions are
currently anticipated to evolve

09:52.340 --> 09:56.390
in a manner that will
warrant only gradual increases

09:56.390 --> 09:59.540
in the target federal
funds rate.

09:59.540 --> 10:02.510
Compared with the
projections made in March,

10:02.510 --> 10:07.120
most FOMC participants
lowered somewhat their paths

10:07.120 --> 10:09.660
for the federal funds rate,

10:09.660 --> 10:12.890
consistent with the revisions
made to the projections

10:12.890 --> 10:17.110
for GDP growth and
the unemployment rate.

10:17.110 --> 10:20.400
The median projection for the
federal funds rate continues

10:20.400 --> 10:24.430
to point to a first
increase later this year,

10:24.430 --> 10:27.920
with the rate rising to about
1 and three quarters percent

10:27.920 --> 10:33.630
in late 2016 and 2 and three
quarters percent in late 2017.

10:33.630 --> 10:38.630
In 2016 and 2017,
the median path is

10:38.630 --> 10:41.520
about one quarter
percentage point below

10:41.520 --> 10:43.900
that projected in March.

10:43.900 --> 10:49.400
The median projected rate
in 2017 remains below the 3

10:49.400 --> 10:52.540
and three quarters
percent or so projected

10:52.540 --> 10:57.080
by most FOMC participants
as the longer-run value

10:57.080 --> 11:01.300
of the federal funds rate even
though the central tendency

11:01.300 --> 11:05.340
of the unemployment rate by
that time is slightly below its

11:05.340 --> 11:09.420
estimated longer-run value
and the central tendency

11:09.420 --> 11:14.820
for inflation is close to
our 2 percent objective.

11:14.820 --> 11:18.680
Participants provided a
number of explanations

11:18.680 --> 11:22.650
for the federal funds rate
running below its normal

11:22.650 --> 11:25.530
longer-run level at that time.

11:25.530 --> 11:29.140
These included, in
particular, the residual effects

11:29.140 --> 11:32.890
of the financial crisis,
which are likely to continue

11:32.890 --> 11:38.050
to constrain spending and credit
availability for some time.

11:38.050 --> 11:41.210
I would like to emphasize
that the forecasts

11:41.210 --> 11:42.660
of the appropriate path

11:42.660 --> 11:45.720
of the federal funds
rate are conditional

11:45.720 --> 11:49.360
on participants'
individual projections

11:49.360 --> 11:53.500
of the most likely outcomes for
economic growth, employment,

11:53.500 --> 11:56.220
inflation, and other factors.

11:56.220 --> 12:00.730
But our actual policy
decisions over time will depend

12:00.730 --> 12:03.850
on evolving economic conditions.

12:03.850 --> 12:07.150
Accordingly, if the expansion
proves to be more vigorous

12:07.150 --> 12:11.080
than currently anticipated
and inflation moves higher

12:11.080 --> 12:15.320
than expected, then the
appropriate path would likely

12:15.320 --> 12:20.250
follow a steeper and higher
trajectory; conversely,

12:20.250 --> 12:23.010
if conditions were
to prove weaker,

12:23.010 --> 12:28.200
then the appropriate trajectory
would be lower and less steep.

12:28.200 --> 12:31.800
Finally, the Committee
will continue its policy

12:31.800 --> 12:36.470
of reinvesting proceeds from
maturing Treasury securities

12:36.470 --> 12:39.530
and principal payments
from agency debt

12:39.530 --> 12:42.030
and mortgage-backed securities.

12:42.030 --> 12:44.250
The Committee's sizable holdings

12:44.250 --> 12:48.220
of longer-term securities should
help maintain accommodative

12:48.220 --> 12:52.310
financial conditions and
promote further progress toward

12:52.310 --> 12:54.200
our objectives.

12:54.200 --> 12:55.190
Thank you.

12:55.190 --> 12:58.370
I'll be happy to
take your questions.

13:00.250 --> 13:10.960
YLAN MUI. Hi, Ylan Mui
from the Washington Post.

13:10.960 --> 13:14.920
As you mentioned, almost all of
the FOMC participants believe

13:14.920 --> 13:17.890
that the first rate hike
will come this year.

13:17.890 --> 13:20.000
But two of your colleagues
believe

13:20.000 --> 13:25.260
that 2016 is the appropriate
time, the IMF has called on 2016

13:25.260 --> 13:27.830
as the appropriate time
for liftoff as well,

13:27.830 --> 13:30.800
and markets seems to place a
greater probability of liftoff

13:30.800 --> 13:33.110
in January than they
do in September.

13:33.110 --> 13:36.680
So what are-what is the
misunderstanding here?

13:36.680 --> 13:39.100
What is-what do they have
wrong, and why do you think

13:39.100 --> 13:42.410
that waiting until
2016 is a mistake?

13:42.410 --> 13:43.270
CHAIR YELLEN.

13:43.270 --> 13:47.250
Well, there are obviously
a range of opinions,

13:47.250 --> 13:54.280
both in the market and among
Committee members, at this time

13:54.280 --> 14:00.270
on what the appropriate
stance of policy is likely

14:00.270 --> 14:03.450
to be later this
year and next year.

14:03.450 --> 14:09.750
But, importantly, the-when the
people write down their dots

14:09.750 --> 14:13.090
in the SEP, they're
making forecasts

14:13.090 --> 14:18.460
about what unfolding
data is likely to show.

14:18.460 --> 14:24.210
But the participants will all
be-their views will evolve

14:24.210 --> 14:26.100
with unfolding data.

14:26.100 --> 14:32.060
For all of us, the appropriate
policy decision is going

14:32.060 --> 14:35.040
to be data dependent, and all

14:35.040 --> 14:37.900
of us will be looking
at incoming data.

14:37.900 --> 14:41.580
And our opinions about
the appropriate timing

14:41.580 --> 14:46.440
of normalization are
likely to shift as we look

14:46.440 --> 14:49.670
at how the data evolves.

14:49.670 --> 14:52.960
Differences in the
appropriate assessments

14:52.960 --> 14:56.540
of the appropriate stance
of policy-in addition

14:56.540 --> 15:00.900
to reflecting different views
on the outlook, there are a set

15:00.900 --> 15:05.840
of risks that all of us
need to weigh in judging

15:05.840 --> 15:08.720
on the-judging the
appropriate time

15:08.720 --> 15:11.270
of the beginning
of normalization.

15:11.270 --> 15:14.470
On the one hand,
waiting too long

15:14.470 --> 15:20.610
to begin normalization can risk
significantly overshooting our

15:20.610 --> 15:23.660
inflation objective
given the lags

15:23.660 --> 15:26.790
in the operation
of monetary policy.

15:26.790 --> 15:28.650
And, on the other hand,

15:28.650 --> 15:34.270
beginning too early could
risk derailing a recovery

15:34.270 --> 15:38.750
that we have worked for a very
long time to try to achieve.

15:38.750 --> 15:42.850
And so we're trying
to assess those risks.

15:42.850 --> 15:44.760
But I want to emphasize,

15:44.760 --> 15:49.590
sometimes too much attention
is placed on the timing

15:49.590 --> 15:53.160
of the first increase in
the federal funds rate.

15:53.160 --> 15:57.910
And what should matter to market
participants is the entire

15:57.910 --> 16:03.160
trajectory, the entire
expected trajectory of policy.

16:03.160 --> 16:07.510
And, again, while our actual
policy decisions will have

16:07.510 --> 16:10.830
to evolve in light of
what really does happen

16:10.830 --> 16:14.480
in the economy, the
Committee, as you can see

16:14.480 --> 16:18.910
by the SEP projections,
currently anticipates

16:18.910 --> 16:23.380
that conditions will evolve
in the economy in a manner

16:23.380 --> 16:24.920
that will make it appropriate

16:24.920 --> 16:29.650
to raise the federal funds
rate gradually over time.

16:29.650 --> 16:31.590
STEVE LIESMAN.

16:31.590 --> 16:32.550
Madam Chair, I wonder

16:32.550 --> 16:36.360
if you might characterize the
progress made towards fulfilling

16:36.360 --> 16:38.190
the Fed's two criteria.

16:38.190 --> 16:41.020
Are you somewhat more
confident, not confident at all

16:41.020 --> 16:43.190
that you're moving
towards 2 percent?

16:43.190 --> 16:45.590
Has there been a lot of
improvement in the labor market?

16:45.590 --> 16:46.790
Some improvement?

16:46.790 --> 16:47.860
And how should we judge

16:47.860 --> 16:50.100
when those two criteria
have been fulfilled?

16:50.100 --> 16:51.140
CHAIR YELLEN.

16:51.140 --> 16:56.120
Well, it's a judgment that the
Committee is-will have to make,

16:56.120 --> 16:59.660
and, as I've said previously and
as we've said in the statement,

16:59.660 --> 17:05.320
it will depend on a wide
range of data and not

17:05.320 --> 17:07.500
on any simple indicators.

17:07.500 --> 17:11.880
So I can't provide you-it
would be wrong for me

17:11.880 --> 17:16.580
to provide you a roadmap that
said something as simple as,

17:16.580 --> 17:19.600
if the unemployment
rate declines to X,

17:19.600 --> 17:25.270
that then the labor market will
have improved enough for us

17:25.270 --> 17:27.380
to begin to raise policy.

17:27.380 --> 17:31.920
Obviously, we have to look
at the pace of job creation.

17:31.920 --> 17:33.570
We have to look at
what's happening

17:33.570 --> 17:38.600
to labor force participation,
to part-time employment

17:38.600 --> 17:44.010
for economic reasons, to job
openings, to the pace of quits,

17:44.010 --> 17:47.540
to wage inflation,
and other indicators

17:47.540 --> 17:49.790
of the state of the
labor market.

17:49.790 --> 17:51.940
I did say when we agreed

17:51.940 --> 17:56.250
that labor market slack has
diminished to some extent

17:56.250 --> 18:02.030
in the intermeeting period,
and, clearly, over a longer span

18:02.030 --> 18:04.460
of time, over the
last several years,

18:04.460 --> 18:07.990
obviously we've made
considerable progress

18:07.990 --> 18:11.300
in moving toward our goal
of maximum employment.

18:11.300 --> 18:15.120
So, in spite of the fact
that there is some progress

18:15.120 --> 18:18.030
on that front, the
Committee wants

18:18.030 --> 18:22.110
to see some further
progress before feeling

18:22.110 --> 18:25.250
that it will be appropriate
to raise rates.

18:25.250 --> 18:30.300
On inflation, again, there
has been some progress,

18:30.300 --> 18:33.820
in the sense energy prices
appear to have stabilized.

18:33.820 --> 18:39.110
Now, inflation is going
to-overall inflation is likely

18:39.110 --> 18:43.660
to run at a low level for a
substantial period of time.

18:43.660 --> 18:48.070
The big declines in energy
prices came toward the end

18:48.070 --> 18:51.110
of last year and the
beginning of this year,

18:51.110 --> 18:54.540
and they're not going to wash
out of the inflation data

18:54.540 --> 18:56.630
until late in this year.

18:56.630 --> 19:00.380
But the fact that energy
prices have stabilized means

19:00.380 --> 19:04.200
that the pressure from
that source is diminishing.

19:04.200 --> 19:08.640
In addition, the dollar appears
to have largely stabilized.

19:08.640 --> 19:12.780
And, with respect to core
inflation, it has been running

19:12.780 --> 19:15.560
under our 2 percent objective,

19:15.560 --> 19:20.950
but declining import prices have
been reducing that pressure.

19:20.950 --> 19:26.410
I believe that as the labor
market continues to improve

19:26.410 --> 19:31.640
and as our confidence in that
forecast rises, at least for me,

19:31.640 --> 19:33.700
my confidence will also rise

19:33.700 --> 19:37.100
that inflation will move
back up toward 2 percent.

19:37.100 --> 19:39.150
I expect that to, over time,

19:39.150 --> 19:41.640
put upward pressure
on core inflation.

19:41.640 --> 19:44.480
JON HILSENRATH.

19:46.370 --> 19:47.900
Thank you, Chair Yellen.

19:47.900 --> 19:51.240
Jon Hilsenrath from the
Wall Street Journal.

19:51.240 --> 19:52.300
Two questions if I may.

19:52.300 --> 19:54.170
First, I wanted to ask
you about a comment

19:54.170 --> 19:57.170
that New York Fed President
William Dudley made recently

19:57.170 --> 19:59.660
that, in retrospect, the Fed
should have raised interest

19:59.660 --> 20:03.340
rates more aggressively
during the 2004-2006 cycle.

20:03.340 --> 20:05.580
This was in a footnote
to a speech he gave.

20:05.580 --> 20:07.190
I wonder if you agree with that

20:07.190 --> 20:10.250
and whether there are any
lessons from the 2004,

20:10.250 --> 20:13.720
2006 cycle that should
be applied today.

20:13.720 --> 20:15.660
And, second, relating
to Congress,

20:15.660 --> 20:18.750
the Fed has resisted
past efforts in Congress

20:18.750 --> 20:23.680
to pass measures like an "Audit
the Fed" bill or a measure

20:23.680 --> 20:25.830
to subject the Fed
to a policy rule.

20:25.830 --> 20:27.460
There's now a Shelby
bill out there.

20:27.460 --> 20:30.380
I wonder if there's anything in
that that you can accept and,

20:30.380 --> 20:33.060
more broadly, whether there's
anything you could point

20:33.060 --> 20:36.140
to that Congress can do to
make the Fed a more effective

20:36.140 --> 20:38.770
institution and a more
accountable institution.

20:38.770 --> 20:39.100
Thank you.

20:39.100 --> 20:40.060
CHAIR YELLEN.

20:40.060 --> 20:43.030
Okay, so on the first
question you asked

20:43.030 --> 20:48.340
about the 2004-06
rate increase cycle.

20:48.340 --> 20:53.400
Throughout that period, the Fed
indicated that rates would rise

20:53.400 --> 20:57.030
at a measured pace, and that
turned out to be, I believe,

20:57.030 --> 21:04.730
17 meetings, with 25 basis
point increases at each meeting.

21:04.730 --> 21:11.950
As I've emphasized, previously,
we have-absolutely do not expect

21:11.950 --> 21:17.150
to follow any mechanical
25 basis points a meeting,

21:17.150 --> 21:22.190
25 basis point every
other meeting,

21:22.190 --> 21:27.250
no plan to follow any type
of mechanical approach

21:27.250 --> 21:29.960
to raising the federal
funds rate.

21:29.960 --> 21:35.520
We will evaluate incoming
conditions and move

21:35.520 --> 21:38.440
in the manner that we
regard as appropriate.

21:38.440 --> 21:40.440
So that's one lesson.

21:40.440 --> 21:46.300
You know, conceivably, I think,
with the benefit of hindsight,

21:46.300 --> 21:50.020
it might have been better
to raise rates more rapidly

21:50.020 --> 21:53.970
or more during the
2004-06 cycle.

21:53.970 --> 21:57.860
You know, I'm not
certain of that judgment,

21:57.860 --> 22:00.720
but I think there's
a case to be made.

22:00.720 --> 22:03.960
You asked about Audit the
Fed and the Shelby bill.

22:03.960 --> 22:11.300
The Shelby bill has a title
in it that addresses a number

22:11.300 --> 22:14.010
of issues pertaining to the Fed.

22:14.010 --> 22:20.360
I suppose I would ask, what
exactly is the problem?

22:20.360 --> 22:26.050
We place high priority
on being an accountable

22:26.050 --> 22:28.620
and transparent central bank,

22:28.620 --> 22:32.710
and I think that if you
compare the transparency

22:32.710 --> 22:37.680
of monetary policy decisions
in the Federal Reserve

22:37.680 --> 22:40.720
with other central banks,

22:40.720 --> 22:44.690
we are one of the most
transparent central banks

22:44.690 --> 22:48.710
in terms of the information
that we provide to the public

22:48.710 --> 22:52.360
in a whole variety of ways.

22:52.360 --> 22:55.930
To my mind, the Fed
is accountable,

22:55.930 --> 23:00.050
and we work well
as an institution.

23:00.050 --> 23:05.660
I'm not certain what the problem
is that needs to be addressed.

23:05.660 --> 23:08.410
SAM FLEMING.

23:08.410 --> 23:09.140
Thanks very much.

23:09.140 --> 23:10.490
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

23:10.490 --> 23:13.410
First question is also
referencing something

23:13.410 --> 23:16.820
that Bill Dudley said recently,
which is to do with the use

23:16.820 --> 23:19.880
of the balance sheet in
tightening monetary policy.

23:19.880 --> 23:23.790
He suggested that the short
rates should be hiked some way

23:23.790 --> 23:28.480
from the zero lower bound before
the Fed continues-considers

23:28.480 --> 23:29.810
ending reinvestments.

23:29.810 --> 23:32.140
I wondered if you could give
a little bit more clarity

23:32.140 --> 23:36.750
on how the Fed intends
to approach the issue

23:36.750 --> 23:39.820
of ending reinvestments
on its balance sheet.

23:39.820 --> 23:43.870
Would you, for instance, see any
argument for tapering the end

23:43.870 --> 23:45.710
to reinvestments to
smooth the profile

23:45.710 --> 23:47.900
of maturities in the portfolio?

23:47.900 --> 23:50.170
The second question is
really on this point

23:50.170 --> 23:52.840
of "gradual"-you've
used this term today

23:52.840 --> 23:53.760
in the opening statement.

23:53.760 --> 23:55.890
Is the term "gradual" on its way

23:55.890 --> 23:58.040
to becoming official
guidance from the Fed?

23:58.040 --> 24:00.580
Is this something we should
start to expect to see popping

24:00.580 --> 24:03.480
up in official FOMC
statements in future?

24:03.480 --> 24:05.090
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

24:05.090 --> 24:08.180
So let me start with
the balance sheet

24:08.180 --> 24:10.730
and our reinvestment policy.

24:10.730 --> 24:16.250
We issued a normalization
statement giving principles

24:16.250 --> 24:23.000
of normalizing policy, and
what we said at that time is

24:23.000 --> 24:31.040
that we expected to reduce or
cease reinvestment at some time

24:31.040 --> 24:35.750
after we had begun the
process of normalizing policy

24:35.750 --> 24:40.030
by raising our target for
the federal funds rate.

24:40.030 --> 24:43.660
We said the timing of that
would depend on economic

24:43.660 --> 24:46.020
and financial conditions,

24:46.020 --> 24:50.800
and the Committee has really
not made any further decisions

24:50.800 --> 24:53.910
about how it's going
to go about doing that,

24:53.910 --> 24:58.480
so President Dudley was
expressing his own personal

24:58.480 --> 24:59.700
point of view.

24:59.700 --> 25:04.460
But this is a matter that the
Committee has not yet decided,

25:04.460 --> 25:07.830
and I can't provide
any further detail.

25:07.830 --> 25:12.530
It's obviously something
we will be thinking about.

25:12.530 --> 25:16.670
Let's see, you asked
about "gradual."

25:16.670 --> 25:22.830
So, in a sense, we
already have a statement.

25:22.830 --> 25:25.850
The last paragraph

25:25.850 --> 25:31.800
of the Federal Open Market
Committee statement says:

25:31.800 --> 25:34.300
"The Committee currently
anticipates that,

25:34.300 --> 25:35.790
even after employment

25:35.790 --> 25:39.400
and inflation are near
mandate-consistent levels,

25:39.400 --> 25:42.390
economic conditions
may, for some time,

25:42.390 --> 25:46.640
warrant keeping the target
federal funds rate below levels

25:46.640 --> 25:49.910
the Committee views as
normal in the longer run."

25:49.910 --> 25:51.650
That's kind of a mouthful.

25:51.650 --> 25:54.760
It's a long sentence,
but I think the spirit

25:54.760 --> 25:59.080
of that sentence is consistent
with my use of the word

25:59.080 --> 26:04.710
"gradual," and it is consistent
with what you see in the Summary

26:04.710 --> 26:06.930
of Economic Projections.

26:06.930 --> 26:10.470
Participants are
projecting-obviously there is a

26:10.470 --> 26:14.850
lot of uncertainty, but
they're projecting increases

26:14.850 --> 26:19.010
that average around 100
basis points per year.

26:19.010 --> 26:21.670
Now, that's not a
promise, it's conditional

26:21.670 --> 26:26.180
on their economic forecasts,
and those forecasts may prove

26:26.180 --> 26:28.280
to be wrong and may change.

26:28.280 --> 26:33.030
But at this time, the assessment
that participants have

26:33.030 --> 26:39.010
of the economy suggest to
them that the appropriate pace

26:39.010 --> 26:42.370
of normalization to keep
the economy on track

26:42.370 --> 26:46.270
to meet our objectives will
be gradual, in that sense.

26:46.270 --> 26:48.560
PETER COOK.

26:53.730 --> 26:59.050
Peter Cook with Bloomberg
Television.

26:59.050 --> 27:02.090
Madam Chair, if I could,
just given your discussions

27:02.090 --> 27:03.800
over the past two days
with your colleagues,

27:03.800 --> 27:05.580
the state of the
economy right now,

27:05.580 --> 27:08.480
the improvements you've seen,
do you think it's still likely

27:08.480 --> 27:11.030
that we'll see a rate
increase this year?

27:11.030 --> 27:13.260
And to follow on that, something
Ylan mentioned-that is,

27:13.260 --> 27:15.420
the comment from the IMF
recently, earlier this month,

27:15.420 --> 27:18.430
and the encouragement you
got from the IMF to hold off

27:18.430 --> 27:20.470
on raising rates
until next year.

27:20.470 --> 27:23.940
Specifically, they said-your
colleague Madam Lagarde

27:23.940 --> 27:26.890
mentioned specifically the
risk-that even though there's a

27:26.890 --> 27:28.690
risk of slight inflation
by doing that,

27:28.690 --> 27:29.530
that there was concern

27:29.530 --> 27:31.930
that a rate hike could
trigger market volatility,

27:31.930 --> 27:33.640
with financial stability
consequences

27:33.640 --> 27:35.740
that go well beyond
the U.S. borders.

27:35.740 --> 27:37.020
How do you respond
to those concerns?

27:37.020 --> 27:40.390
Are you factoring in that
international context

27:40.390 --> 27:41.920
to your decisionmaking?

27:41.920 --> 27:43.950
And was it appropriate for
the IMF to make those kinds

27:43.950 --> 27:45.310
of specific recommendations?

27:45.310 --> 27:46.460
CHAIR YELLEN.

27:46.460 --> 27:52.360
Okay. So your first question was
about a rate increase this year.

27:52.360 --> 27:57.330
You know, again, the Committee
tries to give an indication

27:57.330 --> 28:00.470
in the Summary of
Economic Projections

28:00.470 --> 28:04.780
about how economic
conditions will unfold,

28:04.780 --> 28:07.420
their best projections of that

28:07.420 --> 28:11.910
and what the appropriate
policy will be given in light

28:11.910 --> 28:13.810
of those expectations.

28:13.810 --> 28:19.320
And, clearly, most
participants are anticipating

28:19.320 --> 28:23.740
that a rate increase this
year will be appropriate.

28:23.740 --> 28:28.260
Now, that assumes,
as you can see,

28:28.260 --> 28:32.230
that they're expecting a pickup
in growth in the second half

28:32.230 --> 28:34.570
of this year and
further improvement

28:34.570 --> 28:37.510
in labor market conditions.

28:37.510 --> 28:43.460
And we will all be-we will
be making decisions however

28:43.460 --> 28:48.440
that depend on the actual data
that we see in the months ahead.

28:48.440 --> 28:55.170
So, certainly, we could see
data in the months ahead

28:55.170 --> 29:00.330
that will justify the
expectations that you see

29:00.330 --> 29:02.590
in the so-called dot plot.

29:02.590 --> 29:07.550
But, again, the important point
is, no decision has been made

29:07.550 --> 29:11.940
by the Committee about what the
right timing is of an increase.

29:11.940 --> 29:16.740
It will depend on unfolding
data in the months ahead.

29:16.740 --> 29:20.160
But, certainly, an increase
this year is possible.

29:20.160 --> 29:24.730
We could certainly see data
that would justify that.

29:24.730 --> 29:28.050
In terms the IMF
guidance, you know,

29:28.050 --> 29:33.030
I believe the IMF plays
a very useful role

29:33.030 --> 29:37.590
by undertaking reviews
of the economic policies

29:37.590 --> 29:39.680
of all of its members.

29:39.680 --> 29:45.330
Obviously, there is a range of
opinion among outside observers

29:45.330 --> 29:47.880
and market participants as well

29:47.880 --> 29:53.280
as among the Committee's
participants, as you can see

29:53.280 --> 29:56.880
in the SEP, about how
economic conditions are likely

29:56.880 --> 30:01.220
to unfold and, consequently,
the appropriate timing

30:01.220 --> 30:03.990
of an initial rate hike.

30:03.990 --> 30:08.270
I think, however, we all
agree, and the IMF agrees,

30:08.270 --> 30:11.530
that policies should
be data dependent,

30:11.530 --> 30:14.730
and the Committee is
always doing its best

30:14.730 --> 30:18.680
to assess the implications
of incoming data.

30:18.680 --> 30:22.820
I would point out that
we have had incoming data

30:22.820 --> 30:26.920
since that report was written.

30:26.920 --> 30:28.400
But, again, I want

30:28.400 --> 30:30.830
to emphasize-and I think
the IMF would agree

30:30.830 --> 30:36.020
with this-that the importance of
the timing of a first decision

30:36.020 --> 30:39.980
to raise rates is something
that should not be overblown,

30:39.980 --> 30:42.200
whether it's September
or December

30:42.200 --> 30:47.060
or March-what matters is
the entire path of rates.

30:47.060 --> 30:51.880
And, as I've said, the Committee
anticipates economic conditions

30:51.880 --> 30:55.960
that would call for
a gradual evolution

30:55.960 --> 30:59.470
of the fed funds rate
toward normalization.

30:59.470 --> 31:03.880
With respect to international
spillovers, this is something

31:03.880 --> 31:06.710
that we have been
long attentive to.

31:06.710 --> 31:11.000
Obviously, we have to
put in place a policy

31:11.000 --> 31:15.850
that is appropriate to evolving
conditions in the U.S. economy,

31:15.850 --> 31:20.990
but we can't promise that
there will not be volatility

31:20.990 --> 31:24.060
when we make a decision
to raise rates.

31:24.060 --> 31:29.680
What we can do is to do our
very best to communicate clearly

31:29.680 --> 31:34.780
about our policy and our
expectations to avoid any type

31:34.780 --> 31:39.570
of needless misunderstanding
of our policy

31:39.570 --> 31:43.120
that could create
volatility in the market

31:43.120 --> 31:48.470
and potential spillovers as
well to emerging markets,

31:48.470 --> 31:51.740
and I have been trying to
do that now for some time.

31:51.740 --> 31:55.430
I've been doing my best to
make good on that pledge.

31:55.430 --> 31:57.790
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

32:00.230 --> 32:02.980
Marty Crutsinger with
the Associated Press.

32:02.980 --> 32:05.390
You just talked about the
fact that you can't promise

32:05.390 --> 32:08.010
that there won't be
volatility in the markets.

32:08.010 --> 32:09.560
There seem to be two
schools of thought.

32:09.560 --> 32:14.170
One is that the Fed learned from
the mistakes made in the-2013

32:14.170 --> 32:18.360
with the taper tantrum, and that
you're going to telegraph this

32:18.360 --> 32:20.680
so well that that will limit it.

32:20.680 --> 32:24.260
And the more pessimistic
school of thought seems to be

32:24.260 --> 32:27.250
that when you do start
raising rates, they've been low

32:27.250 --> 32:29.160
for so long that it's going

32:29.160 --> 32:32.680
to be-make the taper tantrum
seem mild by comparison.

32:32.680 --> 32:35.640
Which camp are you
falling in on that?

32:35.640 --> 32:36.940
CHAIR YELLEN.

32:36.940 --> 32:39.890
I think our experience
suggests that it's hard

32:39.890 --> 32:41.940
to have great confidence

32:41.940 --> 32:45.710
in predicting what the
market reaction will be

32:45.710 --> 32:50.330
to Fed decisions, and there
have been surprises in the past.

32:50.330 --> 32:53.690
I don't think the
Committee anticipated

32:53.690 --> 32:59.210
that its decision would
cause the taper tantrum.

32:59.210 --> 33:03.640
And all I can say is that
uncertainty in the markets

33:03.640 --> 33:07.200
at this point about
long-term rates doesn't appear

33:07.200 --> 33:15.890
to be unusually high, and we
can only do what is in our power

33:15.890 --> 33:20.440
to attempt to minimize
needless volatility

33:20.440 --> 33:23.420
that could have repercussions
for other countries

33:23.420 --> 33:27.300
or for financial
stability more generally,

33:27.300 --> 33:32.340
and that is to attempt to
communicate as clearly as we can

33:32.340 --> 33:36.240
about our policy decisions,
what they will depend on,

33:36.240 --> 33:38.920
and what we are looking at.

33:38.920 --> 33:42.270
We will be responding
to incoming data.

33:42.270 --> 33:44.590
We've tried to make that clear.

33:44.590 --> 33:47.890
And I think it's clear that
the market is also responding

33:47.890 --> 33:50.390
to incoming data,
and you can see

33:50.390 --> 33:52.700
that in daily market reactions

33:52.700 --> 33:56.060
to surprises in the
economic data.

33:56.060 --> 34:00.530
And, of course, none of us can
quite forecast what incoming

34:00.530 --> 34:03.790
data will be.

34:03.790 --> 34:07.580
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

34:07.580 --> 34:09.420
Your latest economic
projections show

34:09.420 --> 34:11.480
that you expect the
unemployment rate-or,

34:11.480 --> 34:13.630
many officials expect the
unemployment rate-to fall more

34:13.630 --> 34:16.350
slowly this year and then
to fall, by implication,

34:16.350 --> 34:17.820
more quickly next year.

34:17.820 --> 34:19.880
Could you talk about what has
changed in your assessment

34:19.880 --> 34:24.570
of the labor market and how that
influences the path of policy?

34:24.570 --> 34:25.590
CHAIR YELLEN.

34:25.590 --> 34:32.600
So we are-productivity
growth has been-is a factor

34:32.600 --> 34:38.260
that affects the pace of
improvement in the labor market.

34:38.260 --> 34:42.190
Productivity growth
has been extremely slow

34:42.190 --> 34:49.790
for the last couple of
years, and I think, in part,

34:49.790 --> 34:53.940
the pace of improvement
in the labor market

34:53.940 --> 34:58.310
that we're projecting reflects
the notion that there's likely

34:58.310 --> 35:02.360
to be some pickup in the
pace of productivity growth.

35:02.360 --> 35:05.920
Obviously, that's something
that's quite uncertain,

35:05.920 --> 35:09.120
and it's conceivable that
if productivity growth

35:09.120 --> 35:12.140
disappoints-something I
hope that we won't see,

35:12.140 --> 35:15.310
because that has very
negative implications

35:15.310 --> 35:19.070
for living standards-we
could conceivably see faster

35:19.070 --> 35:21.150
improvement in the labor market.

35:21.150 --> 35:24.220
But, in addition, there
are other margins of slack

35:24.220 --> 35:27.960
that don't show up in
the unemployment rate.

35:27.960 --> 35:32.030
Labor force participation
that has, at least,

35:32.030 --> 35:36.480
is-appears to be depressed,
at least to some extent,

35:36.480 --> 35:41.130
because of cyclical
weakness, and the fact

35:41.130 --> 35:45.560
that labor force participation
rate has remained roughly stable

35:45.560 --> 35:47.520
for the last year or so

35:47.520 --> 35:51.270
when there's an underlying
downward trend suggests

35:51.270 --> 35:57.330
that some slack is being taken
up by, in a sense, improved

35:57.330 --> 36:02.180
or diminished cyclical impact
on labor force participation.

36:02.180 --> 36:05.730
I expect that to continue,
and I would expect also

36:05.730 --> 36:09.150
to see some improvement
in the degree

36:09.150 --> 36:15.670
of part-time employment
that's for economic reasons.

36:15.670 --> 36:21.860
JONATHAN SPICER.

36:21.860 --> 36:23.540
Jonathan Spicer with Reuters.

36:23.540 --> 36:27.890
To your point, Madam Chair, on
needing more decisive evidence

36:27.890 --> 36:31.270
in order to initiate
the first rate hike,

36:31.270 --> 36:35.300
how close do you feel the
economy is to full capacity now?

36:35.300 --> 36:38.620
And given that, you
know, employer costs

36:38.620 --> 36:43.610
and monthly wages are at a pace
that's at a six-year high now,

36:43.610 --> 36:46.050
what is the risk-or,
how does the risk change

36:46.050 --> 36:50.470
that inflation could strengthen
quicker than you're expecting?

36:50.470 --> 36:52.330
CHAIR YELLEN.

36:52.330 --> 36:58.600
So the Committee estimates that
the longer-run normal level

36:58.600 --> 37:03.100
of the unemployment
rate is 5.0 to 5.2.

37:03.100 --> 37:07.750
At 5.5 percent, where we
have an unemployment rate

37:07.750 --> 37:12.710
that still exceeds the
Committee's best attempts

37:12.710 --> 37:16.410
to estimate what is a
normal unemployment rate

37:16.410 --> 37:20.760
for this economy, and, as
I mentioned, there appear

37:20.760 --> 37:26.900
to be unusually large elements
of slack over and above

37:26.900 --> 37:31.090
that in the form of somewhat
still-depressed labor force

37:31.090 --> 37:36.800
participation and part-time
work for economic reasons.

37:36.800 --> 37:40.930
So I think it's fair to say that
most members, most participants

37:40.930 --> 37:45.660
on the Committee, wouldn't judge
yet-although some might-that

37:45.660 --> 37:50.120
most wouldn't judge us to be
at, quote, "maximum employment."

37:50.120 --> 37:54.410
Wage increases are still
running at a low level,

37:54.410 --> 37:57.340
but there have been
some tentative signs

37:57.340 --> 37:59.570
that wage growth is picking up.

37:59.570 --> 38:03.760
We have seen an increase
in the growth rate

38:03.760 --> 38:08.800
of the employment cost index
and a mild uptick in the growth

38:08.800 --> 38:10.820
of average hourly earnings.

38:10.820 --> 38:15.220
I would call these tentative
signs of stronger wage growth.

38:15.220 --> 38:21.210
I think it's not yet definitive,
but that is a hopeful sign.

38:21.210 --> 38:25.340
Still, however, inflation-not
only headline, but,

38:25.340 --> 38:29.240
stripping out food and
energy underlying inflation,

38:29.240 --> 38:34.070
core inflation-is still running
below the Committee's objective.

38:34.070 --> 38:40.740
So I think we need to
see additional strength

38:40.740 --> 38:44.980
in the labor market and the
economy moving somewhat closer

38:44.980 --> 38:49.030
to capacity-the output
gap shrinking-in order

38:49.030 --> 38:52.260
to have confidence that
inflation will move back

38:52.260 --> 38:57.010
up to 2 percent, but we
have made some progress.

38:57.010 --> 39:03.970
GREG ROBB.

39:05.470 --> 39:06.180
Thank you.

39:06.180 --> 39:07.620
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

39:07.620 --> 39:08.920
I'd like to turn your attention

39:08.920 --> 39:12.810
to the housing market
for a question.

39:12.810 --> 39:17.140
Both rents and house prices have
been rising rapidly recently,

39:17.140 --> 39:19.740
squeezing Americans
on both sides.

39:19.740 --> 39:21.830
How comfortable are
you with this,

39:21.830 --> 39:24.220
and does it impact
monetary policy?

39:24.220 --> 39:25.670
Thank you.

39:26.790 --> 39:27.120
CHAIR YELLEN.

39:27.120 --> 39:29.800
Well, I mean, the
house-the increase

39:29.800 --> 39:37.840
in house prices is restoring
wealth of many households

39:37.840 --> 39:40.180
who have that as
their major asset.

39:40.180 --> 39:43.600
It is an important
part of the wealth

39:43.600 --> 39:48.740
of American household-the
American household sector.

39:48.740 --> 39:52.900
And for all of the households
that were underwater,

39:52.900 --> 39:58.710
those house price increases
are improving their financial

39:58.710 --> 40:02.080
condition, although, of
course, at the same time,

40:02.080 --> 40:04.700
it's making housing
less affordable

40:04.700 --> 40:07.030
for those who look to buy.

40:07.030 --> 40:09.670
At the same time,
housing overall,

40:09.670 --> 40:13.650
given the low levels-still
low level of mortgage rates,

40:13.650 --> 40:15.980
remains quite affordable.

40:15.980 --> 40:20.160
I think credit availability
remains quite constrained

40:20.160 --> 40:21.540
for mortgages.

40:21.540 --> 40:27.640
Anyone who doesn't have a
pristine credit rating is-finds

40:27.640 --> 40:32.780
it very difficult at this point
to qualify for a mortgage,

40:32.780 --> 40:36.620
and I think we're seeing
quite a bit of reluctance,

40:36.620 --> 40:40.660
given the job market and
given what the history

40:40.660 --> 40:43.300
of what's happened
to house prices,

40:43.300 --> 40:48.120
of young people to
want to buy homes.

40:48.120 --> 40:51.390
We've seen them delay
marriage and wanting

40:51.390 --> 40:53.530
to have the flexibility to move.

40:53.530 --> 40:58.890
So the demand for multifamily
housing to rent is very high,

40:58.890 --> 41:04.570
and rent prices are moving
up, I think, because of that.

41:04.570 --> 41:07.520
JEREMY TORDJMAN.

41:07.520 --> 41:09.970
Hi, Jeremy Tordjman with
Agence France-Presse.

41:09.970 --> 41:12.050
I have a question on Europe.

41:12.050 --> 41:14.820
As you know, there is a growing
political gridlock on Greece,

41:14.820 --> 41:18.360
and the scenario of a
"Grexit" is getting every day

41:18.360 --> 41:19.390
more likely.

41:19.390 --> 41:23.070
So how concerned are you
by these developments?

41:23.070 --> 41:26.520
Do you feel it could impact the
global and the U.S. economy,

41:26.520 --> 41:30.690
and could it influence in
anywhere-in any way, sorry,

41:30.690 --> 41:34.050
when you will decide to
increase the interest rates?

41:34.050 --> 41:34.760
CHAIR YELLEN.

41:34.760 --> 41:38.340
Well I-unfortunately, Greece
and its creditors are faced

41:38.340 --> 41:42.470
with very difficult and
consequential decisions

41:42.470 --> 41:47.120
at this point and in the
days ahead, and my hope is

41:47.120 --> 41:50.860
that they will continue
to work together to try

41:50.860 --> 41:54.110
to find a solution
to the, you know,

41:54.110 --> 41:56.500
current difficulties
and impasse.

41:56.500 --> 42:01.080
Obviously, European
leaders place great value

42:01.080 --> 42:05.630
on preserving European
monetary, economic,

42:05.630 --> 42:09.470
and political integration, and
the Greek people have made clear

42:09.470 --> 42:14.510
that it's important to them
to remain in the euro area.

42:14.510 --> 42:17.900
So this is a very
difficult situation.

42:17.900 --> 42:21.950
In the event that
there is not agreement,

42:21.950 --> 42:25.100
I do see the potential
for disruptions

42:25.100 --> 42:29.200
that could affect the
European economic outlook

42:29.200 --> 42:31.870
and global financial markets.

42:31.870 --> 42:36.520
I would say that the United
States has very limited direct

42:36.520 --> 42:40.710
exposure to Greece,
either through trade

42:40.710 --> 42:44.630
and financial-or
financial channels.

42:44.630 --> 42:50.010
But to the extent that there are
impacts on the euro-area economy

42:50.010 --> 42:52.530
or on global financial markets,

42:52.530 --> 42:56.440
there would undoubtedly be
spillovers to the United States

42:56.440 --> 42:59.790
that would affect
our outlook as well.

42:59.790 --> 43:03.460
CHRISTOPHER CONDON.

43:05.730 --> 43:08.490
Thank you.

43:08.490 --> 43:10.020
Chris Condon from
Bloomberg News.

43:10.020 --> 43:11.100
Madam Chair, I'd
like to come back

43:11.100 --> 43:13.860
to the topic of consumer
spending.

43:13.860 --> 43:15.880
Consumer spending has
been very disappointing

43:15.880 --> 43:18.500
for many months in
the U.S. economy.

43:18.500 --> 43:22.260
I'm wondering, do you think
there has been a meaningful

43:22.260 --> 43:26.660
shift, and one that will persist
in the behavior of households,

43:26.660 --> 43:29.620
with respect to spending
and savings?

43:29.620 --> 43:31.100
Or would you be more
inclined to look

43:31.100 --> 43:35.640
at the recent more
encouraging retail sales figures

43:35.640 --> 43:40.440
and see perhaps a return of
the American consumer there?

43:40.440 --> 43:41.460
CHAIR YELLEN.

43:41.460 --> 43:48.640
So I think in recent weeks we
have received data that suggest

43:48.640 --> 43:53.460
that consumer spending is
growing at a moderate pace.

43:57.430 --> 44:03.050
I'd say, you know, car sales,
for example, were very strong.

44:03.050 --> 44:06.770
Part of it probably
represents payback

44:06.770 --> 44:12.210
for weak sales during the winter
months, but, nevertheless,

44:12.210 --> 44:16.230
the pace of car sales
has been strong.

44:16.230 --> 44:20.860
And recent readings on
retail sales and on spending

44:20.860 --> 44:26.050
on services have
suggested an improvement

44:26.050 --> 44:28.990
in the pace of consumer
spending.

44:28.990 --> 44:31.560
There are questions
at this point

44:31.560 --> 44:35.480
about just how much
impact we've seen

44:35.480 --> 44:40.020
of lower energy prices
on consumer spending.

44:40.020 --> 44:47.320
The decline in oil prices
translates into an improvement

44:47.320 --> 44:49.720
in household income, on
average, of something

44:49.720 --> 44:55.510
like $700 per household, and I'm
not convinced yet by the data

44:55.510 --> 44:59.650
that we have seen the
kind of response to that

44:59.650 --> 45:02.240
that I would ultimately expect.

45:02.240 --> 45:05.680
And I think it's hard to know
at this point whether or not

45:05.680 --> 45:13.670
that reflects a very cautious
consumer that is eager to add

45:13.670 --> 45:18.240
to savings and to
work down borrowing.

45:18.240 --> 45:21.600
Or, in part, some
survey evidence suggests

45:21.600 --> 45:24.270
that consumers are
not yet confident

45:24.270 --> 45:29.250
that the improvement they've
seen-the decline in their need

45:29.250 --> 45:32.950
to spend for energy, for
gasoline-that that's going

45:32.950 --> 45:35.630
to be something that
will be permanent.

45:35.630 --> 45:38.390
They may think it's
a transitory change

45:38.390 --> 45:40.830
and not yet be responding.

45:40.830 --> 45:42.760
So I think the jury
is out there,

45:42.760 --> 45:47.280
but I think we have seen some
pickup in household spending.

45:47.280 --> 45:48.950
PETER BARNES.

45:51.890 --> 45:53.460
Peter Barnes, Fox Business.

45:53.460 --> 45:56.530
Madam Chair, I wanted to
shift over to the decision

45:56.530 --> 45:59.130
in the AIG case this
past Monday.

45:59.130 --> 46:03.560
And in it, the judge in the
case said in his opinion, quote,

46:03.560 --> 46:05.570
"There is nothing in
the Federal Reserve Act

46:05.570 --> 46:07.400
or any other federal statute

46:07.400 --> 46:09.650
that would permit a
Federal Reserve Bank to take

46:09.650 --> 46:12.860
over a private corporation
and run its business

46:12.860 --> 46:16.620
as if the government
were the owner,

46:16.620 --> 46:18.760
yet that is precisely what
the Federal Reserve Bank

46:18.760 --> 46:23.110
of New York did," and the judge
went on to cite the replacement

46:23.110 --> 46:26.380
of AIG's chief executive
officer and taking control

46:26.380 --> 46:28.710
of AIG's business operations.

46:28.710 --> 46:32.280
I wanted to ask you, did
the Fed break the law

46:32.280 --> 46:36.100
in assisting AIG
back in the crisis?

46:36.100 --> 46:40.420
And if this decision
is upheld on appeal,

46:40.420 --> 46:43.380
how does that affect
the Fed's toolbox?

46:43.380 --> 46:46.940
How does it affect its ability
to help firms in trouble

46:46.940 --> 46:49.020
in a future financial crisis?

46:49.020 --> 46:51.840
Would it make that kind
of assistance illegal,

46:51.840 --> 46:53.060
or would you have
to get Congress

46:53.060 --> 46:55.380
to change the law
and make a fix?

46:55.380 --> 46:56.560
Thank you.

46:56.560 --> 46:57.080
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:57.080 --> 47:01.400
So the Federal Reserve strongly
believes that its actions

47:01.400 --> 47:08.530
with respect to AIG in 2008 were
legal, proper, and effective,

47:08.530 --> 47:15.320
and it believes that they were
necessary, given the threat

47:15.320 --> 47:17.180
that a disorderly failure

47:17.180 --> 47:22.680
of that company would
have likely implications

47:22.680 --> 47:28.600
for the economy, for the
flow of credit to households,

47:28.600 --> 47:32.190
in businesses in the economy.

47:32.190 --> 47:36.620
And it believes that the-we
believe that the terms

47:36.620 --> 47:44.870
of that intervention were
tough, and appropriately so,

47:44.870 --> 47:49.420
in order to protect
taxpayers from the risks

47:49.420 --> 47:52.300
that those rescue
loans presented

47:52.300 --> 47:54.750
at the time they were made.

47:54.750 --> 47:57.230
Now, I should emphasize

47:57.230 --> 48:06.950
that Dodd-Frank changed our
13(3) authority and said

48:06.950 --> 48:14.220
that the Federal Reserve may
not in a future crisis intervene

48:14.220 --> 48:19.760
to attempt to address the
issues of a particular company.

48:19.760 --> 48:24.350
At the same time, it gave the
government a set of new tools

48:24.350 --> 48:30.690
that it could use in a situation
like the AIG situation or Lehman

48:30.690 --> 48:34.080
to try to resolve
such a situation

48:34.080 --> 48:38.190
that poses systemic
risks in an orderly way.

48:38.190 --> 48:43.750
The-I'd just say at this
point, the Federal Reserve

48:43.750 --> 48:48.280
under Dodd-Frank can
continue, if necessary,

48:48.280 --> 48:55.150
in some future crisis to engage
in broad-based programs similar

48:55.150 --> 49:00.840
to the program we had in
effect-the programs we had

49:00.840 --> 49:08.310
in effect in 2008 to provide
support for the issuance

49:08.310 --> 49:12.150
of asset-backed securities
that enabled loans

49:12.150 --> 49:16.250
to small businesses
and to students

49:16.250 --> 49:18.950
and for credit cards
and, you know,

49:18.950 --> 49:21.330
for credit throughout
the economy,

49:21.330 --> 49:25.940
or to support the issuance
of commercial paper.

49:25.940 --> 49:29.790
At this point, I believe we
are working with the Department

49:29.790 --> 49:33.130
of Justice to decide
on next steps.

49:33.130 --> 49:35.010
MARK HAMRICK.

49:38.530 --> 49:41.760
Madam Chair, Mark
Hamrick with Bankrate.com.

49:41.760 --> 49:45.360
So much discussion about
rising rates seems to focus

49:45.360 --> 49:47.150
on the potential
negatives, and I'm wondering

49:47.150 --> 49:48.250
if you could talk a little bit

49:48.250 --> 49:52.090
if you envision some
possible unintended benefits

49:52.090 --> 49:53.420
of higher rates.

49:53.420 --> 49:55.840
And one of the things I'm
thinking about is the fact

49:55.840 --> 49:58.250
that savers have suffered
through so many years

49:58.250 --> 50:02.200
of miserly returns, and many
may be actually anticipating

50:02.200 --> 50:04.340
in a positive way
seeing a better return

50:04.340 --> 50:05.220
on their investment.

50:05.220 --> 50:06.380
Thank you.

50:06.380 --> 50:07.660
CHAIR YELLEN.

50:07.660 --> 50:12.090
So let me say, to my mind,
the most important positive is

50:12.090 --> 50:15.560
that it-I believe a decision

50:15.560 --> 50:20.080
to raise rates would
signify very clearly

50:20.080 --> 50:26.530
that the U.S. economy has made
great progress in recovering

50:26.530 --> 50:29.920
from the trauma of
the financial crisis

50:29.920 --> 50:32.230
and that we're in
a different place.

50:32.230 --> 50:35.530
I think, hopefully,
that would be something

50:35.530 --> 50:37.670
that would be
confidence-inducing

50:37.670 --> 50:41.100
for many households
and businesses.

50:41.100 --> 50:43.840
From the point of
view of savers,

50:43.840 --> 50:47.880
of course this has been
a very difficult period.

50:47.880 --> 50:50.460
Many retirees-and I hear

50:50.460 --> 50:55.870
from some almost every day-are
really suffering from low rates

50:55.870 --> 51:02.080
that they had anticipated would
bolster their retirement income.

51:02.080 --> 51:04.640
This, you know, obviously
has been one

51:04.640 --> 51:09.080
of the adverse consequences
of a period of low rates.

51:09.080 --> 51:14.020
The-you know, we have a good
reason for having kept rates

51:14.020 --> 51:16.910
at the levels that we have.

51:16.910 --> 51:21.100
We-our charge from Congress
is to pursue the goals

51:21.100 --> 51:25.110
of maximum employment
and price stability.

51:25.110 --> 51:26.920
That's what we've been doing.

51:26.920 --> 51:32.230
And obviously there are
benefits from a strong economy

51:32.230 --> 51:37.090
to every household in the
economy, including savers,

51:37.090 --> 51:41.680
from having a better job market
and a more secure economy.

51:41.680 --> 51:45.610
But, yes, when the time
comes for us to raise rates,

51:45.610 --> 51:47.260
I think there will
be some benefits

51:47.260 --> 51:49.800
that flow through to savers.

51:49.800 --> 51:52.210
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

51:55.050 --> 51:58.110
Hi, Jim Puzzanghera
from the L.A. Times.

51:58.110 --> 52:00.930
Just to follow up on that
question, I think a lot

52:00.930 --> 52:03.750
of savers, people on fixed
incomes, were hopeful

52:03.750 --> 52:05.840
that they would see
a rate increase

52:05.840 --> 52:07.710
if not this meeting, soon.

52:07.710 --> 52:11.350
What sort-what kind of
assurances can you give them

52:11.350 --> 52:14.250
and people out there who
think the Fed is never going

52:14.250 --> 52:16.000
to raise rates?

52:16.000 --> 52:18.440
I've got some e-mails
today from people saying,

52:18.440 --> 52:19.590
"They're never going
to raise rates."

52:19.590 --> 52:23.240
What kind of assurance
can you give to people

52:23.240 --> 52:24.860
who are waiting for
that to happen?

52:24.860 --> 52:25.280
CHAIR YELLEN.

52:25.280 --> 52:29.750
I can't give an ironclad
promise, but I think it's clear

52:29.750 --> 52:35.230
from our Summary of Economic
Projections that we anticipate

52:35.230 --> 52:40.070
that the economy will grow, that
the labor market will improve,

52:40.070 --> 52:44.700
that inflation will move
back up to 2 percent

52:44.700 --> 52:49.540
as we-is our objective
over the medium term.

52:49.540 --> 52:54.630
And if economic conditions
unfold in the way that most

52:54.630 --> 52:57.750
of my colleagues
and I anticipate,

52:57.750 --> 53:00.540
we see it as appropriate
to raise rates.

53:00.540 --> 53:03.960
And, as you can see,
the largest number

53:03.960 --> 53:08.270
of participants anticipate
that those conditions should be

53:08.270 --> 53:10.480
in place later this year.

53:10.480 --> 53:12.380
Obviously, we have to-you know,

53:12.380 --> 53:14.990
there can be surprises-that
might not happen.

53:14.990 --> 53:18.950
It's not an ironclad
guarantee, but we anticipate

53:18.950 --> 53:22.270
that that's something that will
be appropriate later this year.

53:22.270 --> 53:28.040
MICHAEL DERBY.

53:28.040 --> 53:29.580
Mike Derby with Dow
Jones Newswires.

53:29.580 --> 53:31.680
Could you shed some
additional light

53:31.680 --> 53:34.980
on how large reverse repo
operations are likely to be

53:34.980 --> 53:36.790
in their initial phase?

53:36.790 --> 53:40.590
And, additionally, how important
are financial conditions

53:40.590 --> 53:43.640
to the pace of the
Fed's tightening cycle?

53:43.640 --> 53:46.020
I mean, how important
is the market's reaction

53:46.020 --> 53:49.390
in determining how fast or
slow and the ultimate endpoint

53:49.390 --> 53:51.070
of a Fed tightening cycle?

53:51.070 --> 53:53.410
CHAIR YELLEN.

53:53.410 --> 53:56.170
So, with respect
to-you asked first

53:56.170 --> 53:59.510
about overnight reverse repos.

53:59.510 --> 54:04.130
And we communicated
in our minutes

54:04.130 --> 54:11.350
that the Committee has
an intention to make sure

54:11.350 --> 54:14.790
that they are
available-overnight repos are

54:14.790 --> 54:19.790
available in large quantity
at liftoff to ensure

54:19.790 --> 54:22.290
that we have a smooth liftoff,

54:22.290 --> 54:25.360
that there will be
an elevated level

54:25.360 --> 54:27.430
of provision of overnight RRP.

54:27.430 --> 54:34.160
However, it is our expectation
and plan that fairly quickly

54:34.160 --> 54:39.240
after liftoff we
will reduce the level

54:39.240 --> 54:44.130
of the overnight RRP facility,
and we have a variety of ways

54:44.130 --> 54:47.100
in which we can do that.

54:47.100 --> 54:51.300
With respect to market
reactions, we always,

54:51.300 --> 54:56.250
in evaluating the economic
outlook, have to take account

54:56.250 --> 55:00.300
of financial conditions,
whether it's the level

55:00.300 --> 55:04.980
of long-term interest rates
or the value of the dollar

55:04.980 --> 55:08.130
in assessing the
economic outlook.

55:08.130 --> 55:11.980
To the extent that there
are market reactions

55:11.980 --> 55:17.680
and market movements, whether
they're in reaction to decisions

55:17.680 --> 55:22.120
of ours or in reaction to
other events-foreign events

55:22.120 --> 55:25.880
or unfolding economic
conditions-we will always take

55:25.880 --> 55:27.960
those into account in deciding

55:27.960 --> 55:30.400
on the appropriate
path of policy.

55:30.400 --> 55:35.230
STEVE BECKNER.

55:35.230 --> 55:42.690
Steve Beckner of MNI.

55:44.780 --> 55:48.190
Madam Chair, good to see you.

55:48.190 --> 55:50.720
You mentioned that the
dollar has stabilized.

55:50.720 --> 55:54.880
And, in fact, since mid-March, I
believe it's given up a good bit

55:54.880 --> 55:56.850
of its gains from last summer.

55:56.850 --> 55:58.970
To what extent now do you think

55:58.970 --> 56:03.320
that there will be an
ongoing drag from the dollar,

56:03.320 --> 56:06.330
taking into account
this dollar retreat?

56:06.330 --> 56:11.280
And, overall, how important
is the dollar exchange rate

56:11.280 --> 56:15.050
in monetary policy these
days relative to the past?

56:15.050 --> 56:16.670
CHAIR YELLEN.

56:16.670 --> 56:18.560
Well, I think we still are,

56:18.560 --> 56:23.370
since last summer-have seen
an appreciable increase

56:23.370 --> 56:27.890
in the value of the
dollar vis-à-vis most

56:27.890 --> 56:31.220
of our trading partners,
including emerging markets.

56:31.220 --> 56:35.130
So it is a significant
appreciation of the dollar,

56:35.130 --> 56:39.420
and I think we have seen that
it's had a negative effect

56:39.420 --> 56:44.560
on net exports and so served
as something of a drag

56:44.560 --> 56:51.320
on the economy, and probably
that drag is going to continue

56:51.320 --> 56:53.580
for some time to come.

56:53.580 --> 56:57.010
So it is a factor
affecting the outlook.

56:57.010 --> 57:03.550
In addition, import prices
for non-oil imports continue

57:03.550 --> 57:07.200
to fall, and I think
that's serving to push

57:07.200 --> 57:11.010
down core inflation
a little bit.

57:11.010 --> 57:14.390
Eventually, I expect
that impact to ebb,

57:14.390 --> 57:17.600
but it is a factor
affecting the outlook.

57:17.600 --> 57:23.790
That said, we obviously have
no target for the dollar.

57:23.790 --> 57:28.720
We take movements in the dollar
and its economic impact as one

57:28.720 --> 57:31.430
of many factors affecting
the outlook.

57:31.430 --> 57:34.720
And, in spite of the
appreciation of the dollar,

57:34.720 --> 57:39.080
the Committee obviously thinks
that the economy is likely

57:39.080 --> 57:42.840
to do well enough
to call-likely call

57:42.840 --> 57:46.070
for some tightening
later this year.

57:46.070 --> 57:49.370
JOHN HELTMAN.

57:49.370 --> 57:51.170
Hi, John Heltman
with American Banker.

57:51.170 --> 57:54.610
I'd like to ask a regulatory
question, if I could.

57:54.610 --> 57:56.810
Last month, Senator
Elizabeth Warren

57:56.810 --> 58:01.030
and Congressman Elijah Cummings
sent a letter to the GAO asking

58:01.030 --> 58:04.610
about an inquiry into the Fed

58:04.610 --> 58:06.350
and other regulators'
implementation

58:06.350 --> 58:09.840
of the Community Reinvestment
Act, a concern being

58:09.840 --> 58:13.710
that the CRA, as
it's implemented now,

58:13.710 --> 58:15.350
is not giving
communities-blighted

58:15.350 --> 58:19.330
communities, like in Baltimore
or other places-enough access

58:19.330 --> 58:21.780
to basic banking services.

58:21.780 --> 58:24.430
Do you think that the Fed
is doing everything it can

58:24.430 --> 58:29.400
in its CRA implementation to
give access to those communities

58:29.400 --> 58:30.990
to these kinds of services?

58:30.990 --> 58:33.500
Do you think it needs
to be doing more?

58:33.500 --> 58:34.780
CHAIR YELLEN.

58:34.780 --> 58:40.470
So we take CRA very
seriously and evaluate

58:40.470 --> 58:44.530
for those banks that
we supervise.

58:44.530 --> 58:49.350
We have a set of guidelines
and are very conscientious

58:49.350 --> 58:54.590
in attempting to
evaluate CRA performance.

58:54.590 --> 58:57.920
It's something that we
certainly take into account

58:57.920 --> 59:03.380
in assessing applications
that we receive for mergers,

59:03.380 --> 59:08.540
and we have very
active programs to try

59:08.540 --> 59:13.650
to bring together
community groups

59:13.650 --> 59:19.210
with banking organizations
to try to provide them

59:19.210 --> 59:23.540
with information about how
they can assess community needs

59:23.540 --> 59:25.320
and best address them.

59:25.320 --> 59:29.950
But we are looking at
CRA and continue to look

59:29.950 --> 59:32.360
to see whether there are ways

59:32.360 --> 59:42.020
in which implementation
could be improved.

